''drum'', G. ''trumm'' (in the pl. ''trümmer'') = Icel. ''thrömr'' (''thram-'') = Norw. ''trom'', ''tram'', ''trumm'', edge, brim, = Sw. dial. ''tromm'', ''trom'', ''trumm'', stump, end of a log (see ''tram''<sup>1</sup>); prob. connected with L. ''terminus'', Gr. τέρμα, term, end, boundary: see ''tram''<sup>1</sup> and ''term''.] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' The fringe of threads which remains attached to a loom when the web has been cut off; also, one of such threads.

{{smaller block|If the colour holde in yarne and ''thrumme'', it will holde much better in cloth. {{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', I. 432.}}}}

{{smaller block|You are not a man; you are not the ''thrum'' of one. Scrape you all up, and we shouldn't get lint enough to put on Chilion's foot. {{right|''S. Judd'', Margaret, i. 17.}}}}

Hence—'''2.''' Any loose thread, or a mass or tuft of loose filamentous material.

{{smaller block|All moss has here and there little stalks, besides the low ''thrum''. {{right|''Bacon'', Nat. Hist., § 537.}}}}

{{smaller block|A child and dead? alas! how could it come?<br>Surely thy thread of life was but a ''thrum''. {{right|''Witts' Recreations'', 1654. (''Nares''.)}}}}

'''3.''' A tuft, or a collection of tufts; a fringe or tassel.

{{smaller block|And tapestries all gold'n-fring'd, and curl'd with ''thrumbs'' behind. {{right|''Chapman'', Iliad, xvi. 220.}}}}

'''4.''' ''pl.'' ''Naut.'', short bits of rope-yarn used for sewing on mats.—'''5.''' ''pl.'' Coarse yarn; waste yarn.—'''6.''' A ragged rocky headland swept by the sea. Also ''thrum-cap''. [Nova Scotia.]—'''Thread and thrum.''' {{smaller|See ''thread''.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Made of thrums, or waste yarn: as, a ''thrum'' cap or hat.

